From: Theorem Labs
Subject: __init_tls: keep a static program's TLS block in .bss, never map it

Upstream sizes builtin_tls for a small block, 16 words, and gets any
larger one from an anonymous mmap during start-up, with no address hint.
In a sanitizer binary that mmap runs before __asan_init, and the kernel
model these binaries are verified against may place an unhinted mapping
at any free address, including inside the shadow the runtime reserves
next with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE.  The reservation then fails with EEXIST,
which is not a refusal the model can filter out, and the sealed runtime
spins.  Measured on 2,038 riscv64 asan/asanubsan binaries: 88 make this
mmap first (every one whose PT_TLS memsz is 136 or more), and with the
block placed at 0x80000000 all 64 of them that carry halting statements
spin.

Make the buffer large enough instead: 131072 bytes of space, against a
largest PT_TLS memsz of 30,369 bytes over every riscv64 binary built
with this toolchain (9,748 ELF files in one build, 9,008 in another).
The buffer is .bss, so the files do not grow; it is part of the image
the kernel maps at exec, below the shadow.  A block that does not fit is
a fatal error, not a fallback mapping: one line to fd 2, then a crash,
before main, on every run.  Raw syscalls, as before: nothing that needs
TLS exists yet.  The line names the capacity, so a tool can read from a
binary's bytes which __init_tls it carries.

This replaces 0002, which checked the result of the mapping that is no
longer made.  Dynamic programs are unchanged (ldso has its own buffer).
Every flavour links this one libc.a, so plain and instrumented programs
both stop mapping.

Applies on top of 0001 (v1.2.6-20, f21a9653).
--- a/src/env/__init_tls.c
+++ b/src/env/__init_tls.c
@@ -36,10 +36,22 @@ int __init_tp(void *p)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/* The main thread's TLS block of a static program lives here, in .bss,
+ * at an address the link fixes.  Upstream reserves 16 words and maps any
+ * larger block with an anonymous mmap at start-up, an mmap with no address
+ * hint that the kernel may place anywhere free: in a sanitizer binary that
+ * includes the range the sanitizer's shadow reservation is about to claim.
+ * This buffer is sized above every block the toolchain is used for, and a
+ * block that does not fit is a fatal start-up error (static_init_tls),
+ * never a mapping. */
+#define BUILTIN_TLS_SPACE 131072
+#define BUILTIN_TLS_STR_(x) #x
+#define BUILTIN_TLS_STR(x) BUILTIN_TLS_STR_(x)
+
 static struct builtin_tls {
 	char c;
 	struct pthread pt;
-	void *space[16];
+	void *space[BUILTIN_TLS_SPACE/sizeof(void *)];
 } builtin_tls[1];
 #define MIN_TLS_ALIGN offsetof(struct builtin_tls, pt)
 
@@ -140,19 +152,18 @@ static void static_init_tls(size_t *aux)
 		+ MIN_TLS_ALIGN-1 & -MIN_TLS_ALIGN;
 
 	if (libc.tls_size > sizeof builtin_tls) {
-#ifndef SYS_mmap2
-#define SYS_mmap2 SYS_mmap
-#endif
-		mem = (void *)__syscall(
-			SYS_mmap2,
-			0, libc.tls_size, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE,
-			MAP_ANONYMOUS|MAP_PRIVATE, -1, 0);
-		/* -4095...-1 cast to void * will crash on dereference anyway,
-		 * so don't bloat the init code checking for error codes and
-		 * explicitly calling a_crash(). */
-	} else {
-		mem = builtin_tls;
+		/* No fallback mapping.  Say why on fd 2 and die: raw
+		 * syscalls, since nothing that needs TLS exists yet.  The
+		 * exit_group is only reached if the store to address 0 did
+		 * not fault. */
+		static const char msg[] = "musl: static TLS block does not "
+			"fit the " BUILTIN_TLS_STR(BUILTIN_TLS_SPACE)
+			"-byte builtin_tls buffer; refusing to map it\n";
+		__syscall(SYS_write, 2, msg, sizeof msg - 1);
+		a_crash();
+		__syscall(SYS_exit_group, 127);
 	}
+	mem = builtin_tls;
 
 	/* Failure to initialize thread pointer is always fatal. */
 	if (__init_tp(__copy_tls(mem)) < 0)
